"oup.silverchair-cdn.com (https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/436/1/807/977018)\nciteturn211view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/436/1/807/977018\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://oup.silverchair-cdn.com/article-minimal/977018; Total lines: 984\nL0: Journal Article\nL1: # Detecting multiple periodicities in observational data with the multifrequency periodogram – I. Analytic assessment of the statistical significance Free\nL2: \nL3: Roman V. Baluev\nL4: \nL5: Roman V. Baluev\nL6: \nL7: Search for other works by this author on:\nL8: \nL9: 【0†Oxford Academic】\nL10: \nL11: 【1†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL12: \nL13: 【2†ADS†adsabs.harvard.edu】\nL14: \nL15: Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society, Volume 436, Issue 1, 21 November 2013, Pages 807–818, 【3†https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stt1617†doi.org】\nL16: \nL17: Published:\nL18: \nL19: 24 September 2013\nL20: Article history\nL21: \nL22: Received:\nL23: \nL24: 20 May 2013\nL25: \nL26: Revision received:\nL27: \nL28: 23 July 2013\nL29: \nL30: Accepted:\nL31: \nL32: 23 August 2013\nL33: \nL34: Published:\nL35: \nL36: 24 September 2013\nL37: ## Abstract\nL38: \nL39: We consider the ‘multifrequency’ periodogram, in which the putative signal is modelled as a sum of two or more sinusoidal harmonics with independent frequencies. It is useful in cases when the data may contain several periodic components, especially when their interaction with each other and with the data sampling patterns might produce misleading results.\nL40: Although the multifrequency statistic itself was constructed earlier, for example by G. Foster in his CLEANest algorithm, its probabilistic properties (the detection significance levels) are still poorly known and much of what is deemed known is not rigorous. These detection levels are nonetheless important for data analysis.\nL41: We argue that to prove the simultaneous existence of all n components revealed in a multiperiodic variation, it is mandatory to apply at least 2^{n} − 1 significance tests, among which most involve various multifrequency statistics, and only n tests are single-frequency ones.\nL42: The main result of this paper is an analytic estimation of the statistical significance of the frequency tuples that the multifrequency periodogram can reveal. Using the theory of extreme values of random fields (the generalized Rice method), we find a useful approximation to the relevant false alarm probability.\nL43: For the double-frequency periodogram, this approximation is given by the elementary formula (π/16)W^{2}e^{− z}z^{2}, where W denotes the normalized width of the settled frequency range, and z is the observed periodogram maximum. We carried out intensive Monte Carlo simulations to show that the practical quality of this approximation is satisfactory. A similar analytic expression for the general multifrequency periodogram is also given, although with less numerical verification.\nL44: methods: analytical, methods: data analysis, methods: statistical\nL45: ## INTRODUCTION\nL46: The raw time series data that are obtained in astronomical or other observations often contain more than one periodic component. For example, in the field of exoplanet discovery, the fraction of known multiplanetary systems is close to 20 per cent, with about 1.3 planets per system on average (see Schneider 2013).\nL47: Although it may be claimed that 20 per cent is still only a small fraction, multiplanetary systems are the objects that are the most interesting for further investigations and the most important for the associated theory.\nL48: However, the complicated character of the compound radial velocity variation that multiple planets induce on their host star sometimes makes such data difficult to analyse. It is well known that multiple periodic variations can interfere with each other and with the periodic patterns of the non-uniform time series sampling.\nL49: When we plot a traditional periodogram of such data, we may even discover that its maximum peak is unrelated to any of the real periodicities (even for data without any random noise at all). Such examples are given, for example, in Foster (1995) and also in the teaching manual Vityazev (2001).\nL50: This obviously occurs because the single-sinusoid signal model, which is implicitly used by the Lomb (1976)–Scargle (1982) periodogram as well as by its more advanced relatives, is inadequate when dealing with data containing two or more periodicities. Note that we do not consider here the overtone harmonics that appear when a single non-sinusoidal periodic signal is involved; for that case, the so-called multiharmonic periodogram (Schwarzenberg-Czerny 1996; Baluev 2009b) should be used.\nL51: We address here a more general case when the frequencies of the harmonics are independent and unknown (not bound to any basic frequency).\nL52: In itself, the definition of a periodogram that can take into account two or more periodicities at once is trivial (see Section 2). We do not claim to have invented it: to our knowledge, the earliest papers utilizing essentially the same multifrequency statistic were Foster (1995, 1996a,b). In these works, the multifrequency test statistic is treated as part of Foster's CLEANest algorithm.\nL53: In fact, the ‘global’ version of the CLEANest method implies dealing directly with the multifrequency periodogram plotted in a multifrequency space (or at least in some representative portions of this space).\nL54: The non-trivial problem that we address here is the estimation of the false alarm probability (FAP) associated with the observed peaks of this periodogram. The FAP is necessary to justify any claims of signal detection in the presence of noise. For the multifrequency periodogram, the FAP characterizes the joint significance of an extracted group of periodicities, and it is different from the usual significances of individual periodicities, each treated on its own.\nL55: We discuss this difference and the practical importance of the joint FAP in Section 3.\nL56: Because the FAP is related to the distribution function of the periodogram maxima, we estimate it with the generalized Rice method for random processes and fields, which allows an efficient approximation to the relevant extreme value distributions. We first used this method for the Lomb–Scargle periodogram in Baluev (2008), and since then it has proved very useful. In Section 4 we derive the FAP formula for multifrequency periodograms using the generalized method described in Baluev (2013a).\nL57: In Section 5 we discuss some further generalizations of the basic multifrequency periodogram, which are analogous to the ones already known for the Lomb–Scargle one. Then, in Section 6, we present the results of Monte Carlo simulations that we carried out to verify our analytic FAP estimations, and in Section 7 we compare the detection sensitivity of single- and multifrequency periodograms.\nL58: Finally, in Section 8 we provide a demonstrative example showing the potential capabilities of the double-frequency periodogram.\nL59: ## MULTIFREQUENCY PERIODOGRAM\nL60: First of all, we define the data set that we deal with. Let it consist of N measurements x_{i}, taken at times t_{i}, and having uncertainties σ_{i}. For now we assume that the σ_{i} are known accurately, although later we will also consider the more usual case when only the weights |$w_i\\propto 1/\\sigma _i^2$| are known, while the values of σ_{i} are known only to a free multiplicative constant.\nL61: The errors of x_{i} are assumed to be Gaussian and mutually independent, with the variances given by |$\\sigma _i^2$|⁠.\nL62: When deriving the definition of the classic Lomb–Scargle periodogram, it is assumed that the data contain only pure noise (the null hypothesis), or noise as well as a signal (the alternative hypothesis). The classic Lomb–Scargle periodogram is based on a simple sinusoidal model of the signal to be detected:\nL63: \nL64: \\begin{equation} \\mu = A \\cos (2\\pi f t) + B \\sin (2\\pi f t), \\end{equation}\nL65: \nL66: (1)\nL67: where the parameters A and B are implicitly estimated by means of a linear regression. Now let us assume that the compound signal, which we expect to reveal in the time series data, is representable as the following sum of n independent sinusoids:\nL68: \nL69: \\begin{equation} \\mu = \\sum _{i=1}^n A_i \\cos (2\\pi f_i t) + B_i \\sin (2\\pi f_i t). \\end{equation}\nL70: \nL71: (2)\nL72: The definition of the multifrequency periodogram itself, based on (【4†2】), is analogous to the one for the Lomb–Scargle periodogram, based on (【5†1】). Here we slightly extend the formulae of a general (but still single-frequency) linear periodogram given in Baluev (2008). Now we have 2n unknown linear coefficients A_{i} and B_{i}. In fact, the frequencies f_{i} are unknown too, but we treat them separately as they are non-linear parameters. Let us rewrite the model (【4†2】) in vectorial notation as\nL73: \\begin{eqnarray} &&{\\mu (\\boldsymbol {\\theta },\\boldsymbol {f},t) = \\boldsymbol {\\theta } \\cdot \\boldsymbol {\\varphi }(\\boldsymbol {f},t),} \\nonumber \\\\ &&{\\boldsymbol {\\varphi }(\\boldsymbol {f},t) = \\lbrace \\cos (2\\pi f_i t), \\sin (2\\pi f_i t) \\rbrace _{i=1,2,\\ldots ,n},} \\nonumber \\\\ &&{\\boldsymbol {\\theta } = \\lbrace A_i,B_i\\rbrace _{i=1,2,\\ldots ,n}, \\quad \\boldsymbol {f} = \\lbrace f_1,f_2,\\ldots ,f_n\\rbrace .} \\end{eqnarray}\nL74: \nL75: (3)\nL76: Now we can eliminate the linear parameters |$\\boldsymbol {\\theta }$| by means of the linear least-squares regression (that is, we estimate them on the basis of the input data x_{i}, t_{i} and σ_{i}). To do this, we must solve the following minimization task:\nL77: \nL78: \\begin{equation} \\chi ^2(\\boldsymbol {\\theta },\\boldsymbol {f}) = \\langle (x-\\mu )^2 \\rangle \\longmapsto \\min _{\\boldsymbol {\\theta }}. \\end{equation}\nL79: \nL80: (4)\nL81: Here we have followed Baluev (2008) in the notation 〈F(t)〉, which denotes the weighted sum of the values F(t_{i}) with the weights |$w_i=1/\\sigma _i^2$|⁠.\nL82: \nL83: Because this χ^{2} function is quadratic in |$\\boldsymbol {\\theta }$|⁠, the necessary minimization is elementary:\nL84: \\begin{eqnarray} &&{\\min _{\\boldsymbol {\\theta }} \\chi ^2(\\boldsymbol {\\theta },\\boldsymbol {f}) = \\langle x^2 \\rangle - \\langle x \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle ^\\mathrm{T} \\langle \\boldsymbol {\\varphi }\\otimes \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle ^{-1} \\langle x \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle ,}\\nonumber \\\\ &&{\\boldsymbol {\\theta }^* = \\arg \\min _{\\boldsymbol {\\theta }} \\chi ^2(\\boldsymbol {\\theta },\\boldsymbol {f}) = \\langle \\boldsymbol {\\varphi }\\otimes \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle ^{-1} \\langle x \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle , } \\end{eqnarray}\nL85: (5)\nL86: \nL87: where ‘⊗’ is the dyadic product of vectors (⁠|$\\boldsymbol a \\otimes \\boldsymbol b$| is a matrix constructed of the elements a_{i}b_{j}). By analogy with the Lomb–Scargle periodogram, the multifrequency periodogram, associated with the model (【4†2】), can be defined as half of the maximum decrement in χ^{2} implied by (【6†5】):\nL88: \\begin{equation} z(\\boldsymbol {f}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\big(\\langle x^2 \\rangle - \\min _{\\boldsymbol {\\theta }} \\chi ^2 \\big) = \\frac{1}{2} \\langle x \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle ^\\mathrm{T} \\langle \\boldsymbol {\\varphi }\\otimes \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle ^{-1} \\langle x \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle . \\end{equation}\nL89: \nL90: (6)\nL91: Because this |$z(\\boldsymbol {f})$| is basically a test statistic, its large values indicate that the data probably contain a variation that can be expressed (or at least approximated) by the model (【4†2】). However, it must be noted that large values of |$z(\\boldsymbol {f})$| do not yet mean that all sinusoidal components of (【4†2】) are actually present.\nL92: When the data contain only a single sinusoid at a given frequency f *, the function |$z(\\boldsymbol {f})$| will be large when f_{i} ≈ f * only for a single index i (for all f_{j} with j ≠ i). In fact, the multifrequency periodogram can also reveal single periodicities too, although its detection power in this case would be smaller than in the single-frequency framework (see Section 7).\nL93: When the data contain n periodicities at |$f_i^*$|⁠, the multifrequency periodogram has a multidimensional cross-like shape: large values at the orthogonal lines |$f_i\\approx f_i^*$| accompanied by an especially high peak at the intersection point. In practice, this picture may of course be made more complicated, for example as a result of the aliasing.\nL94: There are peculiarities of (【7†6】) for the diagonals f_{i} = f_{j}, where the model (【4†2】) becomes formally degenerate. This degeneracy can be easily eliminated, however. For example, for the double-frequency case this can be done by replacing the base |$\\boldsymbol {\\varphi }$| by the following equivalent set :\nL95: \\begin{eqnarray} &&{\\cos (\\pi (f_1+f_2) t) \\cos (\\pi (f_2-f_1) t),} \\nonumber \\\\ &&{\\sin (\\pi (f_1+f_2) t) \\cos (\\pi (f_2-f_1) t),} \\nonumber \\\\ &&{\\cos (\\pi (f_1+f_2) t) \\sin (\\pi (f_2-f_1) t)/(f_2-f_1),} \\nonumber \\\\ &&{\\sin (\\pi (f_1+f_2) t) \\sin (\\pi (f_2-f_1) t)/(f_2-f_1).} \\end{eqnarray}\nL96: \nL97: (7)\nL98: It is not hard to check that an arbitrary linear combination of these new base functions can be transformed to the form of the original model (【4†2】) with n = 2, but now at the line f_{1} = f_{2} we have a non-degenerate base {cos 2πft, sin 2πft, tcos 2πft, tsin 2πft}. This also means that a large diagonal value of |$z(\\boldsymbol {f})$| indicates, in general, a modulated periodicity with a slowly varying amplitude and phase.\nL99: It is possible to further generalize the definition (【7†6】) to deal with some underlying variation in the data that is deemed to always exist, even if the multiperiodic signal that we seek is absent. For example, in practice we must always take into account at least an arbitrary constant offset of x_{i}. We will discuss the ways to further generalize the multifrequency periodogram in Section 5.\nL100: Although some of these generalizations should be treated in practice as mandatory, we do not give the extended definitions here. This is because the definitions here are intended mainly for use in Section 4 below, where we need to deal with simpler formulae.\nL101: The matrix |$\\langle \\boldsymbol {\\varphi }\\otimes \\boldsymbol {\\varphi } \\rangle$| in (【7†6】) is the Fisher information matrix associated with the parameters |$\\boldsymbol {\\theta }$|⁠. In the case of the Lomb–Scargle periodogram, the single non-diagonal element of this matrix could be made zero by choosing a suitable time shift. This simplifies the final formula to a sum of two squared terms. In the case of a double-frequency periodogram, this diagonalization is much harder.\n"
